ultramodern: let the video interface be saved and put back with the memory

A savestate here is the console's eight megabytes plus the registers of every
thread, and the registers are in it for a reason that turns out to have a second
half. A thread of a recompiled game keeps the processor's registers outside the
game's memory, so a memory image alone restores every thread holding somebody
else's register file. The video interface is the same shape of problem. A game
tells it what to do through libultra -- which mode, which framebuffer, whether
the picture is blanked -- and libultra kept that in the VI manager rather than
in the game's memory, so ultramodern keeps it in `events_context.vi`. None of it
is in a memory image either.

For `osViBlack` that is the whole difference between a game you can see and one
you cannot. Every game blanks the screen on its way up and unblanks it on the
frame it first has something to show. A state is taken long after that frame and
restored into a console that has only just booted, so the game resumes in a level
while the interface is still switched off -- and the game will never say
`osViBlack(FALSE)` again, because it said it in a process that is gone. What that
looks like is a game that boots, runs, plays its music, answers the controller
and draws every display list RT64 asks for, behind a window that stays black.
`update_vi` zeroes hStart while VI_STATE_BLACK is set, so the raster is blank and
nothing else about the console is wrong.

So the interface travels with the memory. `save_vi` and `load_vi` are the two
halves of it, and `ViSnapshot` is the state in a shape that can go in a file: the
same fields, with `mode` as an address in the console's memory rather than a
pointer into this process's copy of it, since that is the one field whose value
means nothing to whoever reads the file.

--- a/ultramodern/include/ultramodern/ultramodern.hpp
+++ b/ultramodern/include/ultramodern/ultramodern.hpp
@@ -183,7 +183,47 @@
